# Build configuration for the Bootowser Firefox kiosk build.
#
# Apply with:
#   cp mozconfig-bootowser <firefox-src>/.mozconfig
#   ./mach bootstrap
#   ./mach build
#
# IMPORTANT -- how to edit this file
#
# Every option below has been checked against the *actual* option list of
# Firefox 156 (extract it with `git grep -ho 'option(' -- '*.configure'`, or
# more reliably, just run `./mach configure`: it aborts with
# "Unknown option: --xyz" on the first one that does not exist).
#
# This matters because most of the "safe strip" flags floating around the web
# are stale. Verified absent from Firefox 156's configure:
#
#   --disable-telemetry   --disable-features   --disable-dtd
#   --disable-breakpad    --disable-hubs       --disable-system-addons
#   --disable-experiments --disable-search     --disable-gnome-keyring
#   --disable-gecko-profiling
#   --disable-updater     --enable-webrender   --enable-o2
#   --cache-dir
#
# Passing any of those does not warn and get ignored: configure *aborts the
# entire build*. If you add a flag here, confirm it against the tree first.
#
# Most of what they used to disable is now the default. Firefox moved to
# opt-in feature flags, so "not passing the flag" is what turns the feature
# off. The exceptions are called out below.

# --- The lockdown ----------------------------------------------------------
# Do not remove this line and expect a kiosk. Without it this is stock Firefox
# with a policy file, which is what --kiosk plus policy already gives you.
#
# This defines MOZ_BOOTOWSER, which:
#   * turns on the top-level navigation allowlist in nsDocShell, and
#   * turns on the removal of the Ctrl+O / Ctrl+L / F12 escape hatches.
# See patches/series.
ac_add_options --enable-bootowser

# --- Identity: the browser is Bootowser, not Firefox --------------------------
# --with-app-name is what actually renames the executable: it is MOZ_APP_NAME,
# which lands in application.ini's "Name" field, in dist/bin/<name>, and in the
# Unix remoting socket name. Lower case, because it is a program name.
#
# Three of the other identity variables cannot be set from here:
# MOZ_APP_VENDOR, MOZ_APP_PROFILE and MOZ_APP_UA_NAME are project_flags, and
# configure rejects a mozconfig that tries ("can not be set by mozconfig. Values
# are accepted from: implied"). patches/0003 sets those with imply_option in
# browser/moz.configure, and that patch also rewrites the branding strings under
# browser/branding/ to say Bootowser.
#
# Note what MOZ_APP_UA_NAME=Firefox in that patch is for. Left unset it is empty,
# and the User Agent then falls back to application.ini's "Name" -- so the
# rebrand alone would serve every page "bootowser/156.0" and get the kiosk
# treated as a bot by any site that gates on the UA. Keeping the UA Firefox is
# the one place the rebrand stops, on purpose.
ac_add_options --with-app-name=bootowser

# application.ini "Name" when MOZ_APP_NAME is derived rather than set, plus the
# Windows registry paths and symbolstore PRODUCTNAME. Unused on Linux once
# --with-app-name is set, but leaving it as "Firefox" would leave half the
# tree's own metadata calling itself Firefox.
export MOZ_APP_BASENAME=Bootowser

# --- Optimised, not a debug build ------------------------------------------
# Never a --disable-debug style build: those are for bisecting, not shipping.
# An unoptimised build repaints a kiosk display painfully slowly, which is
# exactly wrong for the one thing this machine does all day.
#
# `--enable-optimize` selects -O2 on desktop targets by default. There is no
# `--enable-o2` option any more; it aborts configure if passed.
ac_add_options --enable-optimize

# ccache. This is opt-in: configure does not look for ccache on its own, it
# only checks for it when asked. Without this line a perfectly good ccache
# install is silently unused and a rebuild after touching a header recompiles
# the world.
#
# The cache *directory* is not configured here on purpose -- Firefox has no
# --cache-dir option any more, so the location is ccache's business. Export
# CCACHE_DIR to put the cache on a filesystem with room:
#
#   export CCACHE_DIR=/mnt/fast/ccache
#
# Do not leave it on the default (~/.cache/ccache) if your root filesystem is
# small; a Firefox ccache runs to several GB.
ac_add_options --with-ccache=ccache

# --- Size and surface-area strips ------------------------------------------
# These are verified to exist in Firefox 156.

# Test suites are the single largest thing in the tree and are worthless in a
# shipping kiosk image.
ac_add_options --disable-tests

# Debug symbols for our own binaries. Not the browser's own symbols -- this is
# the -g flag on things we compile that would otherwise be useless in the field.
ac_add_options --disable-debug-symbols

# WebSpeech exposes the microphone to any page via JS. A kiosk has no user to
# consent to it and no microphone worth opening, and leaving it in is a page-
# reachable capture surface.
ac_add_options --disable-webspeech
ac_add_options --disable-synth-speechd

# wasm sandboxed libraries: this needs a wasi sysroot, which no ordinary
# Arch install provides, so configure dies with "Cannot find wasi headers" if
# it is left on.
#
# Note this is the one genuine security-relevant trade-off in this file, so
# weigh it deliberately: without it, the sandboxed C libraries (the codec /
# image / media decoding helpers) are built as ordinary native code rather
# than as wasm modules with their own reduced syscall surface. For a kiosk
# that only ever loads pages you control, that is an acceptable trade for
# being able to build at all.
#
# If you would rather have the isolation, install a wasi sysroot instead:
#   export MOZ_STDCXX_COMPAT=  # and provide clang's wasm32-wasip1 sysroot
ac_add_options --without-wasm-sandboxed-libraries

# Note: there is no `--disable-update-agent` here, tempting as it looks. It
# exists, but it is declared `when=target_is_windows | target_is_osx` -- the
# update agent is Windows/macOS-only in Firefox 156 -- so on Linux it does not
# merely do nothing, it makes configure abort with
# "--disable-update-agent is not available in this configuration".

# --- Cannot be turned off via configure, and why we care --------------------
# Recorded here so nobody re-adds a dead flag trying to switch them off:
#
#   MOZ_CRASHREPORTER  default-on on Linux x86_64. The only option that
#                      mentions it is --enable-crashreporter; there is no
#                      --disable- form. Policy's DisableTelemetry/
#                      DisableCrashReporter still governs it at runtime.
#   MOZ_UPDATER        same shape: --enable-updater exists, no --disable-.
#   MOZ_NORMANDY       implied on by browser/moz.configure with no CLI switch.
#   MOZ_TELEMETRY_*    keyed off MOZ_TELEMETRY_REPORTING, which defaults to
#                      `mozilla_official` -- false for an unofficial branding,
#                      so it is already off here.
#
# None of these are patched out, because a source patch that deletes the crash
# reporter buys little for a kiosk and costs a lot of merge conflict against a
# moving tree. The runtime policy is the right control for them.

# --- Environment -----------------------------------------------------------
# Rust dependencies come from vendored crates; CARGO_NET_OFFLINE is therefore
# false (online allowed) rather than true, which would fail the vendored
# fetch outright.
export CARGO_NET_OFFLINE=false

# Keep build output outside the source tree so a --clean or a re-clone does
# not fight with it.
mk_add_options MOZ_OBJDIR="@TOPSRCDIR@/../obj-bootowser"